  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThe mid-level ridge is in control of the Central and Southern Plains \nthis morning with a closed upper low still parked across the Pacific \nNorthwest. Less surface wind has allowed temperatures to fall into \nthe low 70s this morning, still about 10 degrees above normal. \nConditions today will be similar to yesterday with temperatures \nquickly warming up as diurnal mixing commences this morning. Highs \nwill reach into the middle 90s to near 100 degrees with heat index \nvalues of 100-106. Something to note is the higher moisture content \naround 700 mb denoted by some short-term guidance. While large-scale \nforcing is lacking, 1000-1500 J/kg of MUCAPE in a weakly capped \nenvironment could (10-15% chance) lead to the development of an \nisolated shower or storm this afternoon. \n\nA backdoor cold front approaches the forecast area on Sunday with \ncloud cover and some precipitation looking more likely. Guidance has \nshown a stronger wave moving around the northern periphery of the \nridge, sparking isolated to scattered showers and storms Sunday \nafternoon. Shear of 25-30kts along with DCAPE of 1100-1300 J/kg \ncould support some strong to severe storms, with damaging wind gusts \nbeing the primary hazard. A complex of storms that develops across \nNebraska Sunday afternoon could move southeast into portions of the \narea by late afternoon into the evening, again bringing some \npotential for strong winds. Cloud cover and showers/storms will lead \nto slightly lower temperatures on Sunday, albeit still hot with \nhighs in the mid to upper 90s and heat index values in the upper 90s \nto around 105 degrees.\n\nUncertainty has increased in temperatures on Monday and will be \ndependent on how convection evolves Sunday evening and overnight. \nCloud cover may stick around through the morning with the potential \nfor a convective cold pool to move through the area as well. If this \noccurs, temperatures may be slower to warm up, but there is good \nconsensus in a southerly wind working to overcome any outflow-cooled \nair and bump temperatures back into the 90s. With temperatures/heat \nindices more marginal on Sunday and Monday for an Excessive Heat \nWarning, there is the potential for some of the warning to be \ntrimmed off, especially closer to the KS/NE stateline where heat \nindices may fall short of 100 degrees. Will not make any changes to \nthe Extreme Heat Warning at this point given uncertainty.\n\nA pattern change comes Tuesday as a potent closed low advances east \nacross southern Canada towards the Great Lakes, shunting a cold \nfront into the central CONUS. Ahead of the front, breezy \nsouthwesterly winds will likely boost temperatures into the upper \n90s to around 100 degrees again Tuesday afternoon with heat index \nvalues of 100-108 degrees. Guidance has come into better agreement \nin this front making it into the forecast area Tuesday evening \nbefore the front hangs up across the region through the end of next \nweek. Chances for showers and storms along the boundary have \nincreased, especially Tuesday night through Wednesday night. This \nalso means confidence in relief from the heat has increased. \nTemperature spreads remain quite large Wednesday through Friday, but \nhave trended down with a long tail on the cool side of the \ndistribution. Even with a slight cool down, temperatures are likely \nto remain above-normal for early September.",
